Absolutely. And I think the real problem isn't that you need **more fancy words**. You need a **small, reliable vocabulary of verbs that describe different kinds of changes**.

Here's a practical commit vocabulary you can keep beside you while working.

# Git Commit Vocabulary

## 🟢 Adding Something New

Use these when introducing a new piece of functionality.

| Verb        | Meaning                                            | Example                               |
| ----------- | -------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| `Add`       | Introduce something new                            | `Add post deletion`                   |
| `Create`    | Create a new file, component, module, etc.         | `Create post model`                   |
| `Introduce` | Bring a new concept or capability into the project | `Introduce authentication middleware` |
| `Implement` | Build functionality according to a requirement     | `Implement user authentication`       |
| `Build`     | Construct a larger feature                         | `Build shopping cart interface`       |
| `Develop`   | Create a more substantial feature/system           | `Develop inventory management system` |
| `Integrate` | Connect an external system/library/API             | `Integrate MySQL database`            |
| `Configure` | Set up configuration                               | `Configure Express server`            |
| `Enable`    | Turn on a capability                               | `Enable EJS view rendering`           |
| `Register`  | Add something to a system's registry/configuration | `Register Express routes`             |

---

# 🔵 Modifying Existing Code

Use these when something already exists but you're changing it.

| Verb      | Meaning                                                 | Example                             |
| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| `Update`  | General modification                                    | `Update Express notes`              |
| `Modify`  | Change existing behavior/code                           | `Modify post validation`            |
| `Adjust`  | Make a small change                                     | `Adjust cart quantity handling`     |
| `Change`  | General behavioral change                               | `Change checkout flow`              |
| `Revise`  | Improve or rewrite existing content                     | `Revise API documentation`          |
| `Extend`  | Add capability to an existing feature                   | `Extend cart with quantity support` |
| `Expand`  | Make functionality broader                              | `Expand product filtering`          |
| `Enhance` | Improve an existing feature                             | `Enhance error handling`            |
| `Improve` | Make something better                                   | `Improve form validation`           |
| `Refine`  | Make an existing implementation cleaner or more precise | `Refine post creation flow`         |

---

# 🟡 Fixing Problems

These are especially useful because they tell future-you **why the commit happened**.

| Verb       | Meaning                                   | Example                                |
| ---------- | ----------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| `Fix`      | Correct a bug                             | `Fix incorrect cart total`             |
| `Resolve`  | Resolve a known issue                     | `Resolve duplicate post IDs`           |
| `Correct`  | Correct incorrect behavior/data           | `Correct product price calculation`    |
| `Repair`   | Restore broken functionality              | `Repair post editing route`            |
| `Handle`   | Add handling for a situation              | `Handle missing post requests`         |
| `Prevent`  | Stop something bad from happening         | `Prevent duplicate cart items`         |
| `Guard`    | Add protection against invalid conditions | `Guard against empty form submissions` |
| `Validate` | Add validation                            | `Validate post creation input`         |
| `Sanitize` | Clean unsafe/unwanted input               | `Sanitize user input`                  |

### ⭐ Especially useful

Instead of:

> `Fix stuff`

say:

> `Fix missing post validation`

Instead of:

> `Update error`

say:

> `Handle missing post with 404 response`

Much more informative.

